# Proxy

A stand-in with the **same interface** as the real subject. Adds caching, access checks, or lazy init **without** changing the subject.

Decorator adds responsibilities (logging, discounts). Proxy **controls access** to the real object (often one wrapper, not a stack of features).

## Anatomy

- Subject interface.
- Real subject (expensive catalog, remote API).
- Proxy implements the same interface and delegates.

```ts
@Injectable()
export class CachingProxyService implements ProductCatalog {
  private readonly cache = new Map<string, Product | null>();

  constructor(private readonly realCatalog: ProductCatalogService) {}

  getProduct(id: string) {
    if (this.cache.has(id)) return this.cache.get(id) ?? null;
    const product = this.realCatalog.getProduct(id);
    this.cache.set(id, product);
    return product;
  }
}
```

Compose proxies with `useFactory` when one proxy should wrap another:

```ts
{
  provide: AccessControlProxyService,
  useFactory: (caching: CachingProxyService) =>
    new AccessControlProxyService(caching),
  inject: [CachingProxyService],
}
```

## Nest mapping

| Need | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| HTTP/GraphQL auth | Guards / pipes (`SpaceAuthorPipe`, `ShareSessionPipe`) |
| In-process cache in front of a slow collaborator | Proxy (or dedicated cache service) |
| Lazy connection | Proxy or `OnModuleInit` |

Do not busy-wait to “simulate slowness”. Do not put a process-wide catalog cache in a singleton unless invalidation is defined (uploads mutate files constantly).

## When to use

- Virtual proxy (cache / lazy).
- Protection proxy when the **subject API** must enforce access, not just the transport.

## When not to use

- Subject is cheap.
- Access is already uniform at the resolver — a guard is enough.
- Proxy would need a different interface → Adapter.

Related: `nest-decorator.mdc`, `nest-adapter.mdc`, `nest-facade.mdc`.
